  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nOngoing significant rainfall and potential flooding over far \nsouthern portions of the CWA should subside soon. However, \ncontinued weak low-level isentropic ascent combined with \nsufficient elevated instability may foster continued development\nacross the southwest CWA through at least daybreak before the \nexisting modest LLJ veers and decays. Will need to monitor if \nany MCV develops within ongoing convection over central Iowa. \nThis would reinvigorate scattered convection with locally heavy \nrain late this morning into the afternoon across, with the most \nlikely MCV track favoring western portions of the CWA.\n\nA robust convective complex currently over south-central \nNebraska is significantly enhancing a mid-level wave across the \ncentral Great Plains. Based on latest guidance, the vast amount \nof convection is expected to turn this wave into a warm core low\nthrough this morning. This wave/low will drift along a broader \nridge through tonight, with latest trajectories favoring a track\nover or slightly north of the forecast area late tonight into \nSaturday morning. Given high-end PWAT values of 2.3\" to possibly\nupward of 2.5\" and expected slow forward motion of the wave, \nsignificant rainfall rates of over 2\" per hour are possible near\nand along the track of the associated surface wave. \nAdditionally, strong southeast winds of advisory caliber (at \nleast 45mph) cannot be ruled out along the southern periphery of\nthe track. A Flood Watch may be needed for areas roughly around\nand north of I-80 for late tonight and Saturday morning. Will \nalso need to monitor a threat for elevated convective linear \nelements capable of transporting corridors of damaging wind to \nthe surface ahead of the wave.\n\nAnother wave expected to become convectively enhanced in the \nlee of the central Rockies tonight will track north of the area \nlate Saturday night, dragging a cold front with showers across \nthe CWA sometime during the daytime hours on Sunday. \nThunderstorm chances will be dictated by the expected timing of \nthe front, with a later arrival in the afternoon supporting \nhigher coverage of storms and a marginal damaging wind risk.\n\nHigh pressure will build southward over the area early next \nweek, supporting seasonable temperatures and a decent chance of \na dry stretch Monday and Tuesday. Another stronger wave and cold\nfront will bring chances of showers and storms on Wednesday, \nwith the front expected to stall well south of the area late in \nthe week. \n\nKluber",
